            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Ecuador\'s Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Human Mobility (MREMH) officially lists "deporte" (sport) as one of a short set of purposes qualifying for an Autorización de Residencia Temporal of up to 180 days, renewable within that period, for foreigners intending to carry out short-term sport, academic, scientific, technological or artistic activity in Ecuador. This is issued through Ecuadorian diplomatic missions abroad or Zonal Directorates within Ecuador — the clearest, most directly confirmed "distinct sport-purpose migration product" found across this ten-country batch.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;">Longer-Term Settlement Route</h4><p>Separately, MREMH also publishes a dedicated "Visa de Residencia Temporal para Deportista, Artista o Gestor Cultural," aimed at foreign nationals who intend to settle (establecerse) in Ecuador specifically to carry out sporting, artistic or cultural activity under regular migratory status — a distinct, longer-term route from the 180-day short-stay authorisation, and more relevant to athletes with an ongoing, longer engagement (e.g. a club contract) rather than a single competition.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;">Professional Footballers Specifically</h4><p>A legal-directory secondary source states that the only visa requirement for a foreign athlete beyond general entry is a labour permit issued by the foreign ministry, but explicitly limits this statement to professional footballers — it should not be read as applying to all sports or as replacing the two official MREMH sport-purpose procedures described above.</p>',
